A twig is a small, weathered line of wood, thin enough to snap between two fingers, yet marked by the entire architecture of the tree from which it fell. Its bark is ridged like old skin. Tiny nodes interrupt its length, each one the sealed memory of a leaf, a flower, or a branch that might have grown. Once, it carried sap upward toward the canopy. Now it lies on the ground, dry and anonymous, while the tree above continues to spread its crown toward the sun.

The modern worker resembles that twig: separated from the living whole, valued chiefly for what can be extracted from it, and discarded when its usefulness appears exhausted. Labor rises through warehouses, offices, kitchens, hospitals, mines, and glowing screens like sap through wood, feeding structures whose highest branches receive the most light. Yet those who keep the system alive are told that their precariousness is a personal failure, while the extraordinary accumulation above them is presented as evidence of genius, discipline, even moral superiority.

The twig is taught to admire the tree’s crown.

Through advertising, celebrity culture, corporate mythology, and the endless theater of luxury, wealth becomes more than possession; it becomes a language of virtue. The mansion signifies intelligence. The private jet signifies courage. The billionaire’s appetite is renamed vision. Workers are invited to identify not with one another, but with the distant possibility that they too might someday reach the canopy. In worshipping the rich, they learn to romanticize the very hierarchy that diminishes them. Their desires are trained upward, away from solidarity and toward imitation.

This is cultural subjugation at its most elegant: domination no longer needs to arrive only as force. It can arrive as aspiration. The exploited person is encouraged to defend the exploiter’s freedom, repeat the exploiter’s values, purchase miniature symbols of the exploiter’s life, and interpret structural deprivation as insufficient ambition. The twig forgets that the tree grew from soil shared by a forest. It imagines the crown created itself.

But wood returns to earth. The hard outline softens. Rain enters the grain. Fibers loosen, darken, and become indistinguishable from the soil around them. From that soil rises something entirely different: a lettuce, low to the ground and open rather than towering, its leaves gathered in pale green layers around a tender center. Each leaf is broad, cool, and faintly veined, holding droplets of water in its folds. It does not reach majestically above the garden. It grows close to other plants, modest and edible, a quiet rosette of crisp leaves unfolding from the common earth.
